   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of Member States to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. MOLISA has developed a Skilling-up Master Plan.  This is an initiative of the Government of Vietnam to standardize and improve skills for Vietnamese workers.
2. The Employment Law was amended including Sector Skills Councils (SSCs) provided the legal basis for the development of sub-laws on SSCs helping promote the sector-based approaches to skills development for better skills matching and allowing for a policy uptake of industries&#x2019; needs, including the textiles and garment sector. 
3.  A package of communication materials such as a skills development factsheet (in English and Vietnamese) for the textiles and garment sector,   published on the ILO website, and a notebook were developed to promote skills needs, skills needs anticipation and skills development for workers in the textiles and garment sector.
 1. ILO provided technical guidance and support to the MOLISA/DVET through the development of the Skilling-up Master Plan and justifications for the revision and amendment of the Employment Law (Chapter on skills development and recognition). The support includes the provision of technical comments and recommendations on the draft Skilling-up Master Plan and organized a technical workshop in collaboration with the MOLISA/DVET to facilitate exchanges, consultation, and inputs from tripartite partners and international experts on the draft. The ILO technical contribution is also with technical comments and recommendations on the appropriate models of SSCs applied for Viet Nam. The submitted Skilling Up Master Plan and the Chapter on skills development in the to-be-amended Employment Law incorporated ILO&#x2019;s inputs. 
The ILO is also providing technical support and guidance to the national consultants from the ILSSA in drafting the Viet Nam national report on LMI for skills and employment policies (intermediate report to serve as inputs into the regional mapping study).</narrative>
   </description>
