  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of Member States to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Tripartite partners and key textile stakeholders joined the consultations held by the ILO in collaboration with MOLISA DVET in November 2022 to identify their needs, expectations, and aspirations on skills needs and share difficulties and challenges faced by the sector. The results served as important inputs for the development of a skills-needs forecasting study and a skills strategy for the sector. MOLISA representatives attended the regional consultations on strengthening LMIS in ASEAN for better skills and employment policies in May 2023. 
2. MOLISA National Institute for Vocational Training and VCCI carried out a training and re-training needs assessment of the textiles, garment, and automotive sectors from 2022. A final report for the activity was completed in October  2023, with the results and lessons learned from the process shared at the regional event.
A stock-taking report on the textiles and garment sector was developed and completed to provide the basis for building a skills-need assessment report which is in the national need. 1. With the support of the Governments of the Netherlands and Japan, the ILO organized and facilitated tripartite consultations and discussions on challenges faced by the textiles and garment sector and skills needs now and in the future in the industries. The consultation results lay an important foundation for developing a skills-needs assessment and a skills strategy for the sector. 
2. The ILO improved knowledge and capacity for national constituents of the skills-needs anticipation approaches and skills mismatch measuring by providing direct technical guidance and support, or through ILO-ITC training courses such as E-learning courses on Measuring Skills mismatch and Skills for a Greener Future. The capacity-building activities enable the tripartite partners to better understand the importance and methods of skills-needs forecasting and skills mismatch measurement which are not frequently applied in Viet Nam, as well as to ensure tripartite partners&#x2019; full participation and engagement in the skills needs study and their engagement in the implementation of its recommendations. 
With the support from GIZ, the ILO also built capacity for participants from MOLISA and the GSO who took part in the capacity building/ knowledge sharing series implemented from May to December 2022, which focused on various areas related to labour market information and skills anticipation and matching. 
The ILO has delivered six capacity-building workshops to the NIVT and VCCI implementing team, including on the use of LFS data for preliminary analysis and sector selection, the development of survey questionnaires, sampling, and survey implementation, using online survey platforms and data cleaning and analysis.</narrative>
   </description>
